rebuilding the hard disk image from source
==========================================

disks/hdinstall/REGEN

README beside this says how to run the install that is here.  This says
how to make the whole thing again from source: a set of floppy images
carrying binaries this tree built - the reconstructed init among them -
from which a hard disk can be formatted, installed and booted the way
the hardware did it.

The four stages are format, install, boot, and the build that feeds
them.  They are independent: a loader can be replaced without redoing
the install, and the install can be redone without reformatting.

None of this is a script yet, deliberately: a script written over the
top of the parts that do not work would hide which those are.

What has been run, as this was written.  The build, all of it.  The
stage 1 command as far as CP/M's A> prompt, with the two floppies and
the drive named the new way - not the format itself.  Stage 3, which
gets as far as the loader and stops where the last section says.  And
the loader replacement below, which is how the rebuilt second level got
onto the disk.  Stage 2 is out of HWSIM.STATUS and the scripts on the
distribution floppy, not from a run here.


what has to be built first
--------------------------

In ~/src/ccc, the compiler and its runtime:

	make all && make install
	sudo make sysinstall

which puts ccc in /usr/local and its headers in both /usr/local/lib/include
and /usr/local/usr/include.

In src/micronix, the system's own headers, which go beside ccc's:

	make sysinstall

That installs into /usr/local/usr/include and leaves ccc's six alone -
cpm.h errno.h hitech.h setjmp.h stdio.h time.h.  The result is one
directory holding both sets, and that is the directory anything built
for the target has to be told about.  It matters here because the
loader's Makefile defaults INC to /usr/local/lib/include, which has
ccc's headers but not types.h or sys/mw.h, so the loader will not
compile without being told:

	make -C src/micronix/stand INC=/usr/local/usr/include bootimg

Then the libraries and the commands:

	make -C src/micronix/lib
	make -C src/micronix/cmd

A trap that has cost time here: make decides what to rebuild from the
dates on the sources, and a new compiler does not touch them.  After a
codegen or ABI change in ccc, every object in the tree is stale while
looking current - and neither lib/libc nor lib/libu has a clean target.
Remove the objects and the archives by hand:

	rm -f src/micronix/lib/libc/*.o src/micronix/lib/libc/libc.a
	rm -f src/micronix/lib/libu/*.o src/micronix/lib/libu/libu.a

cmd does have one: make -C src/micronix/cmd clean.


stage 1: format, under CP/M
---------------------------

The drive is formatted by Morrow's own formatter running under CP/M,
which is booted off the DJ-DMA floppy port:

	cd src/hwsim/d1
	./d1 -b roms/mon447.bin -B djdma \
	    djdma0:../../../disks/dist/1009-8_cold-boot.IMD \
	    djdma1:../../../disks/dist/947-1.IMD \
	    hdcdma0:../../../disks/hdinstall/hddma-0

The cold boot disk carries FORMATMW 1.6, which predates the part number
names: "m5" is rejected there, "seagate" and "st506" are the same drive
and both work.  947-1.IMD carries 1.7 with the full option set, so it
goes in as B: and the command is

	B:FORMATMW M5 SIZE 512

512 because Micronix requires it - see src/hwsim/DISKS for why the two
controllers disagree about sector sizes and what that costs.  The whole
drive formats and verifies in about six seconds.

What a real format leaves in the drive label - the simulator's own,
out of band at the front of the drive file, not anything in the blocks -
worth checking against:

	secsize 512  cyl 153  heads 4  spt 17
	firstsec 0  seccode 3  gap3 43  fill 0xe5  formatted 1


stage 2: install, from the standalone floppy
--------------------------------------------

Boot the 1.67 standalone on the 5 1/4 port, with the formatted drive
attached:

	./d1 -b roms/mon447.bin -B djdma -5 ../../../disks/hdinstall/sa67.5.skew.1k \
	    hdcdma0:../../../disks/hdinstall/hddma-0

Use 1.67 and not 1.6: 1.6's kernel has its root device as djdma/12, the
single sided minor for a disk that is double sided, and it stops in the
rom monitor before reaching a shell.  1.67 has 76.

Wait for the welcome and then for the shell's "#".  Anything typed
before the prompt is thrown away when the shell sets up the terminal,
and a machine that welcomes you and then says nothing may be wedged
rather than waiting - README says how to tell the difference.

The floppy carries the install scripts.  m5init is the one that ships,
and what it does not do is write a boot block; the newer sequence adds
that with mkfs -i, which gives cylinder 0 to a file so the filesystem
cannot allocate over it:

	mkfs -i /bootmw.bin /dev/m5a -1024
	fsck /dev/m5a
	mount /dev/m5a /b
	cptree / /b -v
	setdev /b/micronix 3/0 0/0

cptree is copying a whole filesystem through a simulated Z80, so give
it time.  If it reports "No more space on disk djdma/76" the mount did
not take and cptree is filling the floppy's own /b - start again from a
clean disk rather than tidying up.

setdev replaces the old ddt recipe for pointing the kernel at its root
device.  It reads _rootdev and _swapdev out of the kernel's symbol table
instead of searching for the bytes of djdma/12, which is 1.6's root
device - on a 1.67 kernel that search silently did nothing and every
disk built that way booted a kernel still looking for the floppy.

Leave swap as nodev.  A standalone with no swap device runs entirely in
core, which is what a floppy system wants; pointing swap at the drive
turns a system that does not swap into one that does.  README has the
detail.


stage 3: boot from the hard disk
--------------------------------

	./d1 -b roms/mon447.bin -B hdcdma \
	    hdcdma0:../../../disks/hdinstall/hddma-0

-B hdcdma is the HDC-DMA path with the monitor skipped, which is the
configuration switch 0x0c that everything used to be written down as.
The rom decodes the top five bits at mon447.s:tstsw - 0x00 is the HDCA,
a different controller.

What it produced, the once it worked end to end:

	mwboot:
	Micronix loader for the HD-DMA
	Files:
	micronix
	Loading
	Entering

	Micronix 1.67
	...
	disks: djdma hddma
	root dev: hddma/0
	swap dev: nodev/0
	#


replacing the loader without redoing the install
------------------------------------------------

The loader is two levels: a sector the rom reads to 0100 and jumps to,
and a second level that reads the filesystem to find the kernel.  Both
live in the boot area, which is cylinder 0 - block 5236 on the 5 meg,
because sys/mw.c rotates the mapping and does not put block 0 there -
and the area is owned by /boot/bootmw so nothing allocates over it.

That file can be rewritten from the host, which is much quicker than
reinstalling:

	make -C src/micronix/stand INC=/usr/local/usr/include bootimg
	mnix -f hddma-0 write -k <image> /boot/bootmw

write -k is the important half.  Plain write frees the file first, so
the blocks go back on the free list and come off it again in reverse -
same blocks, same size, boot area written backwards, and nothing in ls
or info shows it.  -k keeps the block list and writes through it.

There is no in-band disk label.  The drive's geometry - what the
machine runs on - is out of band, in a fixed 2k header at the front of
the simulator's drive file: struct disklabel in lib/hdcdmadisk.c,
DATAOFF.  hddma-0 is 5328896 bytes, which is that 2k and then 10404
blocks.  Nothing in the block space describes the drive.

The second half of the boot block is used for something else, and it is
an addition rather than anything Micronix shipped: mkfs's installboot
scribbles format information there, a struct dlabel at DL_OFFSET, 256
bytes into the block the first level occupies.  It holds what the drive
is and where the boot area is:

	d_tracks d_heads d_spt		the geometry
	d_cyl0				bootfirst, where this area starts
	d_bootblks			how many blocks it is
	d_roll				what mw.c adds to blk / spc
	d_fsize d_isize d_swap		the filesystem laid on top

So an image handed to write -k has two things to get right:

  - keep bytes 256-511.  bootimg pads the first level to 512 with
    zeroes and would scribble over the format information.  Splice
    instead: first level, then the existing 256-511, then the second
    level at 512.  The first level is 181 bytes, so it does not reach
    them.

  - pad to the file's full size, 34816, so the size stays what it was
    and all 68 blocks stay claimed.  write -k refuses to grow a file,
    which is the point - a block it had to allocate would not be in the
    area the rom reads.

Worth knowing while stage 3 is broken: the second level does not read
any of that.  stand/*.c mentions neither dlabel nor DL_OFFSET - mwio.c
carries the geometry as a compiled-in default and probes the drive for
the head count - and the probe is what fails.  The answer it is failing
to work out is sitting in the same block it was loaded from, put there
by mkfs for the purpose.  On the image here those bytes are zero,
because mkfs -i has never been run on it.

setblk names the blocks a file owns, for building the arrangement in
the first place.  It does not take them off the free list - run
icheck -s afterwards.


where this stops today
----------------------

Stage 3 does not complete.  The loader runs and reads the disk, and
then:

	mwboot:
	Micronix loader for the HD-DMA
	Files:
	No bootable files

HWSIM.STATUS has the diagnosis: the head probe in stand/mwio.c sends
the same command eleven times - cmd.headsel does not change across the
loop - so the controller answers head 0 every time, the probe matches
only i = 0 and gives up at i = 1, and spec.heads and spec.spc come out
wrong.  Every block number after that lands on the wrong cylinder and
head, so the block read as the root directory is not the root
directory, and nothing in it looks bootable.

An earlier failure, "Block out of range" before the directory read at
all, was a stale loader: the sources had been fixed and the built
objects had not, so the disk carried a loader older than its own fixes.
Rebuilding and reinstalling as above got past it.  If it comes back,
check the dates on stand/*.s and stand/mwboot.com against the sources
before looking anywhere else.

Separately, the image in this directory has no kernel: root holds
micronix.mw with inode 0 - a name with nothing behind it - and no
micronix.  patchm5 on the distribution floppy ends with "ln micronix.mw
micronix; era micronix.mw", so a half-run patch leaves exactly that.
Even with the probe fixed there would be nothing to boot until stage 2
is run again.


what is still missing for the stated goal
-----------------------------------------

The goal is a set of floppy images carrying binaries built here.  The
build produces them and the install consumes them, and the join between
is not made: standalone167.bin is the distributed 1.67 image, and it is
skewed - its geometry comes from the minor number in the name it is
reached by, bdev(2,93), and bit 3 of that minor selects the skew.  mnix
reads and writes an unskewed filesystem, so it cannot put anything into
that floppy: pointed at it, fsize reads 52481 on a 400k image and the
root inode looks like a character device.

So there are two ways forward and neither has been taken:

  - teach mnix the skew, so the distributed floppy can be written the
    same way the hard disk image is, or

  - build a floppy image here rather than patching one: mkfs a blank,
    populate it with mnix, and give it a minor whose geometry matches.

Until then the binaries this tree builds can be put onto the hard disk
image directly - that is how the rebuilt init got there - but not onto
the floppy that installs it.

